From 6313caeb0cbf7c867ecf7b33f8723f87cbab5f26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 07:23:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the output that native output takes is
 captured whole and given back verified

The class that held the output captured a fixed list of properties and put each back whatever
had happened to it. It now captures every property of the connector, the CRTC
and the plane in use that is not read-only (a blob by its bytes, of which a
copy is kept), except those that something else owns, that cannot be put back
or that are not a state, each named with its reason (OUTPUT_STATE::Exclusion()).
What the transport sets is named by the caller and validated against the
definition of each property (enum values, ranges).

Giving the output back:
- only what is not as it was found is put in the request, from what the kernel
  has now, and a value that its own property would not take is never asked for;
- the colour policy and the signals that the transport set go in every attempt,
  whole; a commit that the driver refused is tested again change by change
  (TEST_ONLY) and what it would not take, of what the transport did not set, is
  left out and said;
- the output is read back once the commit is taken, what is not as found is set
  again in a commit of its own, and what could not be is logged exactly;
- the objects are told by their ids, so an output that was unplugged and found
  again is the same output, and another display is not.

What the transport made of the output is also kept in /run/hades-dv-state
(written whole, with a checksum). A Kodi that ended during native output has the
properties that are still as the transport made them put back with the first
modeset of the next one, and those that somebody changed since are left alone;
the defaults of the driver are staged for what the record does not name, as
before.

The decision logic is OutputState.h, which does not need libdrm; a test builds
it on the host against a model of a KMS driver (objects, flags, blobs with the
lifetime that the kernel gives them, TEST_ONLY and commit, a driver that
rejects or alters values). The handover class (OutputHandover.h/.cpp), OutputState.h and
LibdrmKms.h are written from the KMS interface and are GPL-2.0-or-later.
